Post2Plate is a recipe inbox for food creators who live on Instagram and TikTok and have no website, no SEO and no email list. You DM or share a post, or paste a link or the text. It pulls the caption, transcribes the video's audio, parses it into a structured recipe and publishes it at `/@handle/recipe-name`, with a cook mode on the web.

I built it solo over two weeks in May 2026, with Claude Code and Codex agents writing most of the code. Two calls shaped it. Halfway through, the plan flipped from a private iOS app to public web pages. And the parser got permission to refuse, because a parser that always succeeded was inventing recipes.

I did the product, the brand, a FastAPI and Postgres backend, a Next.js front end and a Figma plugin. 258 commits between May 2 and May 16.

## Same product, flipped in one day

The first plan was iOS-first and private by default, aimed at blog creators who already earn ad revenue from recipe sites. Success meant a user importing three recipes in a week.

On May 11 the PRD, the roadmap and the design system all got rewritten in one day. The PRD's own words for the old plan: going after blog-ad revenue was "strategically scary and tactically slow." The better opening was to be the first thing a social-first creator owns. That changed three things.

Web came first. Dynamic Island, Live Activities and App Clips moved to phase 2.

Recipes went public by default. A private library hides the creator's recipes from their own audience, which defeats the point. The owner can still hide one.

Success got redefined. Three imports only proves someone wants to save recipes. Cooking one proves saving them paid off. The new target was at least one cook plus three imports in 14 days.

Handles come from the social account that sent the DM, so a guest's recipes stay private until that person claims the account. I deferred comments, likes, follows and embeds on purpose. The bet is that fans cook and re-cook. Likes don't measure that, and embeds pull traffic away from the page.

