## A parser that's allowed to say no

The 2025 version of this project, which I threw out, returned a hardcoded pasta recipe whenever an import failed, so the demo always "worked." The rewrite bans that kind of fake success in CI.

The parser got the same treatment. In the first calibration run on 10 posts, 100% of imports produced a saveable recipe, but only 40% matched the hand-checked answers. The model was inventing ingredient lists from promotional captions. So I added a hard refusal: no draft unless the post has at least three named ingredients and two ordered steps. The save rate fell to 50%, which meant it was finally measuring something. The hand labels were wrong too. They'd been written from captions alone while the parser also read the audio transcript, so 7 of the 10 "wrong" answers were really labeling mistakes.

Eight models went through the same 10 posts and four tied at 100%. The cheapest and fastest was gpt-oss-120b on Cerebras, at about half GPT-4o's cost per post. On 60 real posts, Cerebras with GPT-4o as a fallback and Whisper for audio matched the hand labels 92% of the time and never refused a real recipe. GPT-4o alone scored 90% at twice the cost. The fallback kicks in on 13% of posts.

I also dropped OCR on video text overlays. It mattered on 1 of 61 posts and added cost, plus noise from stickers, on every one. With OCR off and a faster hosted Whisper, the production setup ran all 60 posts for 19 cents.

## Three scores that were wrong

Around the parser sit about ten small, cheap models, the kind that count word patterns instead of calling an LLM. They handle things like ingredient roles, substitutions, cooking temperatures and step timing. Their labels came from public recipe datasets, from larger LLMs, and from transcripts of people actually cooking.

Two days after the first scorecard, a diagnostic run took three of its numbers apart.

The temperature model scored 0.751, but the input text contained the temperature. With that removed and the model retrained, it scored 0.636. Always guessing the most common answer scores 0.602.

The substitution model's jump was credited to 321 examples from cooking videos. All 321 were positive examples. On the original test rows, the real gain was 0.7 points, which is noise.

The method-fix model passed its promotion gate. Its 500 training labels all had the same value, and a model that always gives that one answer scored exactly the same 0.685. A constant passed the gate. Its honest ceiling later was 0.36.

The ingredient-matching model, at 0.991, got the same check and held up: always guessing the common answer scores 0.753. After that week every score carried a confidence range, gates were set relative to that dumb-guess baseline, and synthetic examples were only allowed in training data. The journal's version of the rule is to fix the labels instead of the metric.

{% Figure src="/images/projects/post2plate/score-check.svg" alt="Bar chart. Temperature model: 0.751 reported, 0.636 after removing the leak, 0.602 for always guessing. Method-fix: 0.685, identical to always guessing. Ingredient matching: 0.991 against a 0.753 always-guess baseline." caption="Two of these scores were barely better than a constant. The third held up." width="wide" /%}

## What isn't real yet

The small models run in shadow mode only, and no live traffic has produced a shadow result yet. Their confidence scores aren't trustworthy enough either: the worst calibration error is 0.124 against a 0.05 gate. TikTok won't let the bot send DMs, so TikTok is receive-only. The acknowledgement DM takes about 7.4 seconds against a 2-second target. Run end to end with no cache, the 60-post set dropped to 50%, because the scraper's free tier ran out of memory. That wasn't a parser miss, and a yt-dlp fallback fixed it. Stripe isn't integrated. The 200-user beta in the roadmap is a target, and I don't have numbers from it.

